## Proof

**Yes**, the formal inverse $1/f$ converges absolutely in a neighborhood of $0$.

Write $f = c_0 + h$, where $c_0 = f(0) \neq 0$ and $h \in \mathbb{C}[[z_1,\dots,z_n]]$ has zero constant term ($h(0)=0$). The formal inverse is

$$
\frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{c_0}\cdot\frac{1}{1+h/c_0} = \frac{1}{c_0}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(-\frac{h}{c_0}\right)^k,
$$

which is a well-defined formal power series since $h$ has no constant term (each coefficient of the partial sums stabilizes in finitely many steps). This is exactly the formal inverse $g = 1/f \in R$, characterized by $f\cdot g = 1$.

Now use convergence. Since $f$ converges absolutely in a neighborhood of $0$, so does $h = f - c_0$. Being an absolutely convergent power series, $h$ defines a continuous (indeed holomorphic) function with $h(0)=0$. Hence there is a polydisc $D_s = \{|z_i|<s_i\}$ on which $h$ converges absolutely and $|h(z)| < |c_0|/2$ for all $z\in D_s$.

On $D_s$, the geometric series $\sum_{k\ge 0}(-h(z)/c_0)^k$ has ratio bounded by $1/2$, so it converges absolutely and uniformly on compact subsets. Its sum is a holomorphic function on $D_s$, equal to $1/f(z)$ pointwise. Expanding this holomorphic function as a power series at $0$ recovers the formal series $g$ (by uniqueness of the formal inverse: the formal product $f\cdot g=1$ forces the Taylor coefficients of any analytic inverse to match $g$). Therefore $g = 1/f$ converges absolutely in $D_s$.

